WEBVTT

NOTE Timed from the narration (kokoro-v1.0 af_heart x1.0).

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.233 line:96%,end align:center
Technology is changing the
way people work around the

2
00:00:03.233 --> 00:00:06.033 line:96%,end align:center
world, and the Philippines
is no exception.

3
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.267 line:96%,end align:center
As automation and artificial
intelligence become more common,

4
00:00:10.267 --> 00:00:14.133 line:96%,end align:center
Filipino workers face both new
opportunities and challenges.

5
00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:19.667 line:96%,end align:center
I’m Vanessa Salonoy, and this research
examines how workplace automation

6
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:24.367 line:96%,end align:center
affects Filipino workers,
compares five possible responses,

7
00:00:24.367 --> 00:00:26.900 line:96%,end align:center
and evaluates one proposed solution to

8
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.233 line:96%,end align:center
help workers transition into new roles.

9
00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:33.367 line:96%,end align:center
Through published evidence, a
worked example and measurable

10
00:00:33.367 --> 00:00:37.667 line:96%,end align:center
criteria, I’ll explain why career
conversion deserves consideration

11
00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:41.567 line:96%,end align:center
and how its success could be
evaluated in the Philippines.

12
00:00:43.967 --> 00:00:47.933 line:96%,end align:center
My research looks at technology
as a driver of globalization,

13
00:00:47.933 --> 00:00:51.867 line:96%,end align:center
reaching Filipino workers through
the global trade in services.

14
00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:57.333 line:96%,end align:center
The IT and business process
management industry, or IT-BPM,

15
00:00:57.333 --> 00:01:00.900 line:96%,end align:center
has helped make the Philippines
a global outsourcing hub,

16
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.667 line:96%,end align:center
and international clients
increasingly expect providers

17
00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:07.567 line:96%,end align:center
to use AI to cut costs
and raise productivity.

18
00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:12.400 line:96%,end align:center
An International Labour
Organization study estimates that

19
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.800 line:96%,end align:center
12.7 million Filipino workers,
more than a quarter of

20
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.933 line:96%,end align:center
employment, are in occupations
exposed to generative AI.

21
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.467 line:96%,end align:center
Exposure means some tasks could be
automated, not that the job is lost.

22
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:29.533 line:96%,end align:center
Only 3.6 percent of jobs fall in the
highest displacement risk category.

23
00:01:30.433 --> 00:01:32.300 line:96%,end align:center
The change is also uneven.

24
00:01:32.567 --> 00:01:35.633 line:96%,end align:center
Women face roughly double
the exposure of men,

25
00:01:35.633 --> 00:01:39.100 line:96%,end align:center
young workers are more often in
occupations at greater displacement

26
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.200 line:96%,end align:center
risk, and around two in five jobs in the

27
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.600 line:96%,end align:center
National Capital Region are exposed.

28
00:01:44.833 --> 00:01:47.700 line:96%,end align:center
And while training is funded,
I found no scheme that

29
00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.267 line:96%,end align:center
replaces a mid-career worker’s
income while they retrain.

30
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.367 line:96%,end align:center
So my position is that automation
should continue, but with support built

31
00:01:56.367 --> 00:02:00.867 line:96%,end align:center
into the transition, not offered after
workers have already lost ground.

32
00:02:03.267 --> 00:02:06.733 line:96%,end align:center
To find a response, I
compared five alternatives:

33
00:02:06.733 --> 00:02:10.267 line:96%,end align:center
two Philippine programmes,
national reskilling and JobStart,

34
00:02:10.267 --> 00:02:13.433 line:96%,end align:center
and three from Singapore:
job redesign, training

35
00:02:13.433 --> 00:02:16.233 line:96%,end align:center
credits and place-and-train
career conversion.

36
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.000 line:96%,end align:center
At this Determining Solution stage,
I judged each one on four criteria I

37
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.833 line:96%,end align:center
selected: sustainability, impact,
feasibility and effectiveness.

38
00:02:26.067 --> 00:02:29.567 line:96%,end align:center
I placed the greatest emphasis
on effectiveness in my final

39
00:02:29.567 --> 00:02:32.800 line:96%,end align:center
judgement, because my question
is whether workers are actually

40
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:37.133 line:96%,end align:center
supported, although I did not
assign numerical weights or scores.

41
00:02:38.033 --> 00:02:40.567 line:96%,end align:center
Place-and-train career
conversion ranked first.

42
00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:45.167 line:96%,end align:center
It is the only alternative with a
published control-group evaluation,

43
00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:48.867 line:96%,end align:center
and the only one that keeps workers
earning while their role changes.

44
00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:51.433 line:96%,end align:center
Its limitation here is feasibility,

45
00:02:51.433 --> 00:02:54.467 line:96%,end align:center
because I found no Philippine
funding line for conversion.

46
00:02:54.733 --> 00:02:57.733 line:96%,end align:center
National reskilling ranked
second: the most feasible

47
00:02:57.733 --> 00:03:00.633 line:96%,end align:center
starting point, but with
no published results yet.

48
00:03:00.867 --> 00:03:04.700 line:96%,end align:center
JobStart ranked third because
it only serves 18 to 24

49
00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:08.000 line:96%,end align:center
year olds with less than a
year of work experience.

50
00:03:08.267 --> 00:03:12.667 line:96%,end align:center
Job redesign and training credits
ranked lower, mainly on feasibility.

51
00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:15.767 line:96%,end align:center
For these four,
effectiveness is not shown,

52
00:03:15.767 --> 00:03:20.033 line:96%,end align:center
which means I found no published outcome
evaluation, not that they failed.

53
00:03:20.933 --> 00:03:22.600 line:96%,end align:center
Three things must stay separate.

54
00:03:22.833 --> 00:03:27.067 line:96%,end align:center
Singapore’s existing programme hires
a jobseeker into a new growth role

55
00:03:27.067 --> 00:03:32.267 line:96%,end align:center
first, then trains them on the job or
through industry-recognised training.

56
00:03:33.167 --> 00:03:36.933 line:96%,end align:center
Its published evaluation found
that, compared with control groups,

57
00:03:36.933 --> 00:03:43.133 line:96%,end align:center
participants earned 2.5 percent more
in the placement year and 5.8 to 6.5

58
00:03:43.133 --> 00:03:45.767 line:96%,end align:center
percent more over the
next four years, with

59
00:03:45.767 --> 00:03:49.033 line:96%,end align:center
retention 3.9 percentage points higher.

60
00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:51.233 line:96%,end align:center
These are Singapore results only.

61
00:03:52.133 --> 00:03:54.633 line:96%,end align:center
My proposal is a Philippine adaptation of

62
00:03:54.633 --> 00:03:57.000 line:96%,end align:center
that model, which does not exist yet.

63
00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:03.867 line:96%,end align:center
Here is how it would work for Maria,
a hypothetical contact centre agent.

64
00:04:04.133 --> 00:04:07.767 line:96%,end align:center
Parts of her role are automated,
but her job is not removed.

65
00:04:08.667 --> 00:04:11.600 line:96%,end align:center
Her employer refers her to the
local Public Employment Service

66
00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:16.100 line:96%,end align:center
Office, or PESO, which assesses
her against roles in demand.

67
00:04:16.333 --> 00:04:19.433 line:96%,end align:center
A participating employer then
hires her into a new role before

68
00:04:19.433 --> 00:04:23.567 line:96%,end align:center
she is fully trained, and trains
her, alone or with a training

69
00:04:23.567 --> 00:04:28.667 line:96%,end align:center
provider, under Republic Act
12063, the enterprise-based

70
00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:31.833 line:96%,end align:center
training law, which already
covers employed workers.

71
00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:35.067 line:96%,end align:center
A proposed training allowance
would keep her earning,

72
00:04:35.067 --> 00:04:38.667 line:96%,end align:center
and her completion, retention and
wage change would be recorded.

73
00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:43.233 line:96%,end align:center
The Department of Labor and Employment
and the PESOs would deliver the

74
00:04:43.233 --> 00:04:48.200 line:96%,end align:center
track, TESDA would register the
training, and the ICT department and the

75
00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:52.500 line:96%,end align:center
IT-BPM industry association
would identify growth roles.

76
00:04:52.733 --> 00:04:56.200 line:96%,end align:center
None of them has been approached,
and no budget has been allocated.

77
00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:02.400 line:96%,end align:center
Now the most important question:
how success would be evaluated.

78
00:05:02.633 --> 00:05:06.467 line:96%,end align:center
The four Evaluating Success
criteria share their names with my

79
00:05:06.467 --> 00:05:10.567 line:96%,end align:center
comparison criteria, but here
they test my own proposal.

80
00:05:10.833 --> 00:05:14.700 line:96%,end align:center
Every target I mention is a planning
target I set, not a result.

81
00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.867 line:96%,end align:center
Sustainability asks
whether the solution could

82
00:05:17.867 --> 00:05:20.467 line:96%,end align:center
stay viable and keep benefiting workers.

83
00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:25.267 line:96%,end align:center
I rate it moderate to high, because
it would run on existing systems:

84
00:05:25.267 --> 00:05:29.433 line:96%,end align:center
the enterprise-based training law
and the PESOs that deliver JobStart.

85
00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:33.567 line:96%,end align:center
The weakness is funding,
as the 740 million peso

86
00:05:33.567 --> 00:05:37.033 line:96%,end align:center
IT-BPM commitment covers only three years.

87
00:05:37.267 --> 00:05:41.533 line:96%,end align:center
My SMART indicators do not include
a dedicated sustainability measure.

88
00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:46.333 line:96%,end align:center
Retention at twelve months is only
a proxy for lasting outcomes,

89
00:05:46.333 --> 00:05:48.033 line:96%,end align:center
so continued funding and employer

90
00:05:48.033 --> 00:05:51.067 line:96%,end align:center
participation would be
judged qualitatively.

91
00:05:51.967 --> 00:05:54.000 line:96%,end align:center
Impact asks what workers would gain.

92
00:05:54.233 --> 00:05:57.600 line:96%,end align:center
I rate it high in principle,
but narrow at first:

93
00:05:57.600 --> 00:05:59.667 line:96%,end align:center
workers keep an income while they convert.

94
00:05:59.933 --> 00:06:04.067 line:96%,end align:center
It would be assessed by wage change,
with no real wage decrease for at

95
00:06:04.067 --> 00:06:08.200 line:96%,end align:center
least 80 percent of completers, by
whether places reach the most exposed

96
00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:12.000 line:96%,end align:center
groups, and by a worker survey
setting a first baseline.

97
00:06:12.233 --> 00:06:14.367 line:96%,end align:center
But a first cohort would
reach only a small

98
00:06:14.367 --> 00:06:18.233 line:96%,end align:center
fraction of the 12.7
million exposed workers.

99
00:06:19.167 --> 00:06:23.033 line:96%,end align:center
Feasibility asks what implementation
would need in the Philippines.

100
00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:24.600 line:96%,end align:center
I rate it moderate.

101
00:06:24.867 --> 00:06:29.233 line:96%,end align:center
Employed workers are already eligible
for enterprise-based training,

102
00:06:29.233 --> 00:06:30.933 line:96%,end align:center
and employers with registered programmes

103
00:06:30.933 --> 00:06:33.667 line:96%,end align:center
can claim an additional tax deduction.

104
00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.767 line:96%,end align:center
Still missing are an
income-support rule for mid-career

105
00:06:36.767 --> 00:06:40.667 line:96%,end align:center
trainees and a way to match
displaced workers to new roles.

106
00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:43.533 line:96%,end align:center
Employer participation would be the test,

107
00:06:43.533 --> 00:06:46.267 line:96%,end align:center
with a target of 30
firms in the first year,

108
00:06:46.267 --> 00:06:48.333 line:96%,end align:center
because the model depends
on employers hiring

109
00:06:48.333 --> 00:06:51.067 line:96%,end align:center
first, and none has agreed to that yet.

110
00:06:51.967 --> 00:06:53.933 line:96%,end align:center
Effectiveness asks whether the programme

111
00:06:53.933 --> 00:06:55.900 line:96%,end align:center
would achieve its intended outcomes.

112
00:06:56.167 --> 00:07:00.567 line:96%,end align:center
It is not yet shown here, because the
gains so far are Singapore results.

113
00:07:00.833 --> 00:07:04.500 line:96%,end align:center
A Philippine pilot would be
judged against its own targets:

114
00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:08.067 line:96%,end align:center
75 percent of a first cohort
completing the cycle,

115
00:07:08.067 --> 00:07:10.967 line:96%,end align:center
70 percent still in the
new role six months after

116
00:07:10.967 --> 00:07:14.367 line:96%,end align:center
completing it, and wage
change against previous pay.

117
00:07:14.633 --> 00:07:17.167 line:96%,end align:center
Completion is not employment, and with no

118
00:07:17.167 --> 00:07:20.333 line:96%,end align:center
Philippine baseline, the
first cohort would set one.

119
00:07:21.233 --> 00:07:25.000 line:96%,end align:center
In short, effectiveness and
impact have direct indicators,

120
00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:27.533 line:96%,end align:center
feasibility has one, and sustainability is

121
00:07:27.533 --> 00:07:30.267 line:96%,end align:center
assessed qualitatively, with one proxy.

122
00:07:32.667 --> 00:07:35.133 line:96%,end align:center
To conclude, automation
is changing Filipino

123
00:07:35.133 --> 00:07:38.400 line:96%,end align:center
jobs more than removing
them, but unevenly.

124
00:07:38.633 --> 00:07:41.533 line:96%,end align:center
I selected a Philippine
adaptation of Singapore’s

125
00:07:41.533 --> 00:07:44.767 line:96%,end align:center
place-and-train model because
it has the strongest evidence

126
00:07:44.767 --> 00:07:48.400 line:96%,end align:center
of the five and keeps workers
earning while they retrain.

127
00:07:48.667 --> 00:07:52.433 line:96%,end align:center
It would build on the training laws,
employer incentives and employment

128
00:07:52.433 --> 00:07:56.833 line:96%,end align:center
offices the Philippines already
has, adding what I did not find:

129
00:07:56.833 --> 00:07:59.100 line:96%,end align:center
employer-linked career conversion with

130
00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:01.433 line:96%,end align:center
income support for mid-career workers.

131
00:08:01.667 --> 00:08:03.533 line:96%,end align:center
Its success would be
judged against the four

132
00:08:03.533 --> 00:08:06.800 line:96%,end align:center
criteria and the SMART
measures, not assumed.

133
00:08:07.667 --> 00:08:11.600 line:96%,end align:center
The full research, its sources and
its limitations are on the website.
